const articleHtml = "<p>A bird DNA report arrives as one word: male, female, detected, not detected. Behind it sit assumptions about sample identity, DNA survival, primer specificity, calling thresholds and control performance. When one fails, the report can be wrong in two directions. A false positive asserts a target that is not there. A false negative misses one that is. Inconclusive is neither.</p>\n<p>This article sits under the guide to <a href=\"/bird-dna-testing-methods-accuracy/\">bird DNA testing methods and accuracy</a> and applies to CHD sexing, pathogen PCR and parentage work.</p>\n<h2>The Two Ways a Bird DNA Test Can Be Wrong</h2>\n<p>A false positive asserts presence. In the CHD test described by Griffiths and colleagues, the positive signal is normally CHD-W, so a false positive turns a ZZ male into a ZW female. In pathogen testing it turns a clean sample into a detection.</p>\n<p>A false negative asserts absence. A female is scored male because the W product never appeared. An infected bird is scored negative because the target sat below the assay's reach or the reaction was suppressed.</p>\n<p>The consequences are not symmetrical. A bird sold as the wrong sex can wreck a pairing plan; one wrong studbook entry can distort generations of records. A pathogen false positive can trigger isolation, while a false negative can let an infection spread.</p>\n<p>Sensitivity is the share of true positives an assay calls positive; specificity is the share of true negatives it calls negative. Both belong to a defined assay, species panel and sample type. Prevalence shapes what a positive means. At a 1:1 sex ratio a positive call tracks specificity. At 1% prevalence, a 99%/99% assay flags roughly as many false positives as true positives.</p>\n<h2>False Positive and False Negative, Defined</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Report</th>\n<th>Actual state (female = positive)</th>\n<th>Classification</th>\n<th>Typical cause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Female</td>\n<td>Female (ZW)</td>\n<td>True positive</td>\n<td>Not an error</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Female</td>\n<td>Male (ZZ)</td>\n<td>False positive</td>\n<td>Female DNA contamination, non-specific band, heteroduplex artifact, sample swap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Male</td>\n<td>Male (ZZ)</td>\n<td>True negative</td>\n<td>Not an error</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Male</td>\n<td>Female (ZW)</td>\n<td>False negative</td>\n<td>CHD-W dropout, co-migration, inhibition, degraded sample, primer mismatch</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The female-as-positive convention is common but not universal; a qPCR assay may report a Z/W ratio or a Ct difference. The labels change, not the logic.</p>\n<p>In sexing, \"negative for CHD-W\" is the male pattern only when CHD-Z amplified and the sample passed its adequacy check. A tube with no amplification is a failed test, not a male result.</p>\n<h2>Contamination: The Leading Route to a False Positive</h2>\n<p>Contamination is the usual explanation for a male reported female, and for a clean sample reported positive. It arrives by three routes.</p>\n<p><strong>Sample-to-sample contamination.</strong> Two feathers in one envelope, a shared pair of scissors, a glove that touched one bird and then another. Female DNA supplies the W target, and a few cells can be enough after 35 to 40 cycles.</p>\n<p><strong>Carryover contamination.</strong> Amplicon from an earlier PCR batch enters a new reaction. It is short, abundant and already bounded by the primer sites, so it amplifies efficiently. Laboratories separate pre-amplification work from post-amplification work for this reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Reagent and environmental contamination.</strong> A shared buffer, a contaminated water batch, a splashing positive control, dust from an earlier extraction. This pattern often appears in the no-template control or across unrelated samples at once.</p>\n<p>The signature is context. One male sample with a faint second band, surrounded by clean male lanes and a clean no-template control, may be a heteroduplex or a non-specific product. A batch where three unrelated samples carry the same unexpected band, or where the extraction blank is positive, points to contamination.</p>\n<p>Prevention is procedural: separate benches, unidirectional workflow, aliquoted reagents, bleach or UV, negative controls, and a rule that a positive no-template control invalidates the batch. The <a href=\"/sample-shipping/\">sample shipping guide</a> covers handling outside the lab.</p>\n<h2>Non-Specific Amplification and Heteroduplex Artifacts</h2>\n<p>Non-specific amplification means the primers extended from an unintended site: low annealing temperature, too many cycles, or a primer with a near-match elsewhere in the genome. The product can be close enough in size to the target that a gel looks plausible.</p>\n<p>Signs include a band at the wrong size, extra bands, a smear, a melt peak at the wrong temperature in a SYBR assay, or late amplification in samples that should be blank. A laboratory that counts bands without a size standard and known male and female controls cannot separate CHD-W from an artifact of similar size.</p>\n<p>Heteroduplexes are the subtler version. When a reaction contains two related sequences, such as CHD-Z and CHD-W, or two variants of CHD-Z, strands can separate at the final denaturation and reanneal to the wrong partner. The mismatched duplex has a different shape and often migrates more slowly, appearing as an extra band, a doublet or a smear.</p>\n<p>That matters because an extra band in a male lane looks like CHD-W. Casey and colleagues documented the failure in a standard avian sexing protocol: heteroduplex molecules caused male birds to be scored as females (Molecular Ecology Resources, 2009). The assay produced a product that mimicked the female pattern.</p>\n<p>Band count is not a diagnosis. A validated assay states the expected fragment sizes for the species, primer set and platform, and what to do with an unexpected band: repeat, add a reannealing step, switch to <a href=\"/how-capillary-electrophoresis-analysis-works-in-bird-sexing-and-parentage-testing/\">capillary electrophoresis</a>, or confirm by sequencing.</p>\n<h2>Allele Dropout: The Quiet Route to a False Negative</h2>\n<p>Allele dropout is the failure of one target to amplify while another target in the same reaction succeeds. In bird sexing the target that drops out is usually CHD-W. The result is a female that looks male: a strong Z band and no W band.</p>\n<p>The causes are ordinary: a W primer site that differs by a base or two, a longer W amplicon that fragments first, a few copies of W decided by stochastic sampling, an inhibitor that suppresses the weaker reaction. PCR favors the shorter product.</p>\n<p>Dropout is not co-migration. With co-migration the W product is present but runs at the same size as Z, so the gel shows one band. With dropout the W product was never made. Co-migration can be solved by better separation; dropout needs a different primer, a cleaner extract or a new sample.</p>\n<p>Low-template samples are the usual setting. Taberlet's work on very low DNA quantities established the multiple-tube approach: amplify the same extract several times and accept a result only when the replicates agree (Nucleic Acids Research, 1996). A single amplification from a shed feather is thin evidence for a male call. The same logic runs through STR parentage, where a dropped allele can create an apparent exclusion, a false negative for the true parent. For the CHD system and its limits, see the <a href=\"/the-complete-guide-to-bird-dna-sexing/\">complete guide to bird DNA sexing</a>.</p>\n<h2>Inhibition, Degradation, and Sample Quality</h2>\n<p>Inhibition is chemical: something in the extract blocks the polymerase. Feathers carry melanin, blood carries heme, swabs carry polysaccharides and bile salts. Extraction reagents can leave guanidine, ethanol or EDTA behind if the washes are incomplete.</p>\n<p>A partly inhibited reaction does not always fail. It may amplify later, with a lower peak, or with a skewed ratio between two targets. That is enough to turn a female into a one-band male pattern, or a low-load pathogen into a negative. Severe inhibition produces no amplification at all. An internal amplification control is the only way to tell.</p>\n<p>Degradation is physical: DNA is cut into pieces, and longer amplicons fail first. A feather left in a hot car, a swab shipped dry, a blood tube thawed twice, or formalin-fixed tissue reduces intact template. Degradation rarely creates a false positive; it creates weak results, dropout and inconclusive reports.</p>\n<p>Sample quality sets the ceiling. A freshly plucked feather with a visible follicle, dried and shipped dry, is close to ideal. A shed feather from the cage floor may contain no follicular cells. Blood collected in heparin can inhibit PCR; EDTA is the better anticoagulant for DNA work. An unvalidated swab medium can dilute the sample or add inhibitors. The <a href=\"/how-feather-pcr-testing-works-for-pbfd-and-avian-polyomavirus/\">feather PCR guide</a> covers collection for pathogen work.</p>\n<h2>Quality Control Failures and Sample Identity</h2>\n<p>Controls answer specific questions. A no-template control asks whether reagents and environment are free of amplifiable target. An extraction blank asks whether extraction introduced DNA. A positive control asks whether the assay amplified a known target. An internal control asks whether this extract supports amplification.</p>\n<p>None asks whether the sample in tube 47 belongs to the bird on the form. Chain of custody fails in ordinary ways: two birds sampled at once, labels in the same hand, racks loaded in a different order, a cap that comes off inside a bag. The signature is a result that contradicts breeding records, or the same genotype in two samples. In parentage work a swap produces multiple Mendelian inconsistencies, not one.</p>\n<p>A control failure can invalidate a batch that looks fine. If the positive female control shows one band, the assay failed to amplify W that day, and every male call in the run is suspect. If the no-template control shows a band, the run is contaminated, and every positive is suspect. The report should name the controls and state whether they passed. The <a href=\"/avian-molecular-biology-guide-the-foundation-of-bird-dna-testing/\">avian molecular biology guide</a> explains what those controls measure.</p>\n<h2>Negative, Inconclusive, and Invalid Are Not Synonyms</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>What the laboratory is saying</th>\n<th>Evidence behind it</th>\n<th>What it does not mean</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Negative</td>\n<td>The target was not detected above the assay's limit of detection</td>\n<td>Valid run, controls pass, adequacy control amplifies</td>\n<td>The bird is certainly free of the target</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inconclusive</td>\n<td>The available evidence cannot support a classification</td>\n<td>Equivocal signal, disagreeing replicates, inadequate sample, or an unresolved control failure</td>\n<td>The bird is negative, male or clean</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Invalid</td>\n<td>The run or the sample failed; no result exists</td>\n<td>Control failure, processing error, broken chain of custody</td>\n<td>Anything about the bird</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In sexing, a male call needs a Z product and no W product under a valid assay. If neither amplifies, the correct category is inconclusive, not male. This is the most common reporting error in low-quality work, and the customer cannot see it because \"male\" looks like an answer.</p>\n<p>For pathogen testing, a negative needs a passing adequacy control. If the internal control fails, the sample is inconclusive even when the target lane is blank. A negative states only that the target was below the limit of detection. The <a href=\"/avian-gender-dna-test/\">avian gender DNA test</a> page shows how a validated sexing report is assembled.</p>\n<h2>What Breeders and Clinics Can Do Before and After Testing</h2>\n<p>Before the sample leaves: pluck feathers with visible follicle tissue, keep one bird per envelope, wear gloves, label the envelope before the feather goes in, dry the sample and ship promptly.</p>\n<p>Ask the laboratory four questions. Which primer set or probe assay do you use for this species? Has it been validated for this species, and on what reference samples? What controls run with each batch, and will the report show them? What is your inconclusive rate for this sample type?</p>\n<p>After a surprising result, do not act on it alone. Ask for the control status and the reason for the call. Resample from a different feather tract. If the question is co-migration, <a href=\"/capillary-electrophoresis-vs-traditional-gel-electrophoresis-which-should-your-bird-dna-lab-use/\">capillary electrophoresis versus gel electrophoresis</a> explains why the platform matters. If the question is dropout, a new extract from a fresh feather is more useful than a repeat of the same tube. For a pathogen positive, repeat with a new sample and interpret alongside clinical signs and history; PCR detects nucleic acid, not necessarily a live organism or disease.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Can a bird DNA test be wrong?</strong>\nYes. Validated assays are reliable, but contamination, sample quality, genetic variation and interpretation can produce a wrong call.</p>\n<p><strong>What is the most common cause of a male bird being reported female?</strong>\nFemale DNA contamination is the usual explanation, followed by heteroduplex artifacts and non-specific bands that resemble CHD-W.</p>\n<p><strong>What is the most common cause of a female bird being reported male?</strong>\nCHD-W dropout, often in a low-template or degraded sample. Fragment co-migration produces the same one-band pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Does a second test always fix a wrong result?</strong>\nNo. A repeat from the same extract can reproduce the same artifact. A new sample from a different feather tract gives independent evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Can a negative pathogen test be wrong?</strong>\nYes. A low pathogen load, a sampling site that missed the target, intermittent shedding, inhibition or degradation can all produce a false negative.</p>\n<p><strong>How do I tell a negative result from an inconclusive one?</strong>\nRead the control statement. A negative has a valid run and a passing adequacy control. An inconclusive result has equivocal evidence or a failed control.</p>\n<h2>The Author's Take</h2>\n<p><strong>Position.</strong> A false result is usually an evidence problem, not a technology problem. The most useful thing a laboratory can publish is not an accuracy figure but its control policy, its species validation and its retest rules.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning.</strong> The same CHD assay can be reliable in one species and misleading in another, and the same sample can pass one day and fail the next. What separates a trustworthy report from a plausible one is whether the laboratory can show the controls, explain the calling rule and say what it does when the evidence is thin.</p>\n<p><strong>Disclosure.</strong> This is the author's editorial judgment. It is not a laboratory certification, a guarantee of any individual result, or veterinary advice. Clinical decisions belong with a veterinarian who knows the bird.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Griffiths, R., Double, M. C., Orr, K., &amp; Dawson, R. J. G. (1998). A DNA test to sex most birds. <em>Molecular Ecology</em>, 7(8), 1071–1075. https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1365-294x.1998.00389.x</li>\n<li>Morinha, F., Cabral, J. A., &amp; Bastos, E. (2012). Molecular sexing of birds: a comparative review of PCR-based methods. <em>Theriogenology</em>, 78(4), 703–714. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.theriogenology.2012.04.015</li>\n<li>Casey, A. E., Jones, K. L., Sandercock, B. K., &amp; Wisely, S. M. (2009). Heteroduplex molecules cause sexing errors in a standard molecular protocol for avian sexing. <em>Molecular Ecology Resources</em>, 9(1), 61–65. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1755-0998.2008.02307.x</li>\n<li>Çakmak, E., Akın Pekşen, Ç., &amp; Bilgin, C. C. (2017). Comparison of three different primer sets for sexing birds. <em>Journal of Veterinary Diagnostic Investigation</em>, 29(1), 59–63. https://doi.org/10.1177/1040638716675197</li>\n<li>Schrader, C., Schielke, A., Ellerbroek, L., &amp; Johne, R. (2012). PCR inhibitors: occurrence, properties and removal. <em>Journal of Applied Microbiology</em>, 113(5), 1014–1026. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1365-2672.2012.05384.x</li>\n<li>Kwok, S., &amp; Higuchi, R. (1989). Avoiding false positives with PCR. <em>Nature</em>, 339(6221), 237–238. https://doi.org/10.1038/339237a0</li>\n<li>Taberlet, P., et al. (1996). Reliable genotyping of samples with very low DNA quantities using PCR. <em>Nucleic Acids Research</em>, 24(16), 3189–3194. https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/24.16.3189</li>\n</ol>";
